\
How the GC works, finalizers, dispose pattern, the different generations, LOH, heap fragmentation and compaction, that the stack doesn't get garbage collected and why, GC pauses \uc0\u8232 
\f0\b\fs32 4. Le LOH (Large Object Heap) et la fragmentation\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls56\ilvl0
\f2\b0\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Qu'est-ce que c'est ?
\f1\b0  Tous les objets dont la taille d\'e9passe 
\f0\b 85 000 octets
\f1\b0  (comme les grands tableaux ou les gros tampons) ne vont pas dans les g\'e9n\'e9rations classiques, mais directement dans le 
\f0\b LOH
\f1\b0 .\
\ls56\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Le probl\'e8me de la fragmentation :
\f1\b0  Pendant longtemps, le LOH n'\'e9tait 
\f0\b pas compact\'e9
\f1\b0  par le GC (pour des raisons de performance, car d\'e9placer de gros blocs m\'e9moire co\'fbte cher en CPU).\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls56\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 R\'e9sultat : \'e0 force de cr\'e9er et d\'e9truire de gros objets, la m\'e9moire se remplissait de "trous" (fragmentation).M\'eame s'il restait assez de m\'e9moire totale libre, il devenait impossible d'allouer un nouveau gros objet d'un seul tenant, provoquant une erreur OutOfMemoryException.\
\ls56\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f3\i\fs26 (Note : Depuis .NET Core, le GC peut compacter le LOH \'e0 la demande, mais cela reste une op\'e9ration lourde \'e0 \'e9viter).
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 5. Les pauses du GC (GC Pauses / STW - Stop The World)\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 Pour nettoyer la m\'e9moire en toute s\'e9curit\'e9 sans qu'un autre thread ne vienne modifier les objets pendant qu'il les analyse,le GC doit parfois mettre 
\f0\b tous les threads de l'application en pause
\f1\b0  (ph\'e9nom\'e8ne appel\'e9 
\f3\i Stop-The-World
\f1\i0 ).\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls57\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 L'impact :
\f1\b0  C'est la cause principale des micro-ralentissements (
\f3\i jank
\f1\i0  ou latences) impr\'e9visibles dans les applications \'e0 haute performance.\
\ls57\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Comment les minimiser ?
\f1\b0  R\'e9duire drastiquement les allocations sur le heap (en utilisant des struct, Span<T>, et ArrayPool<T> comme vu pr\'e9c\'e9demment) pour que le GC ait beaucoup moins de travail \'e0 faire.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \
Understand strings in depth (2 bytes/character Unicode) string encoding (surrogate pairs, uft-8 differences), problems that can go wrong with unicode (orphaned surrogate) and how to fix. Understand that strings are one of the most memory intensive types and can keep the GC busy if you don't know what you're doing\uc0\u8232 Understanding strings in .NET in depth requires looking past the simple syntax and examining how characters are stored in memory, how Unicode works under the hood, and why strings are notorious for keeping the Garbage Collector (GC) busy.\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 1. The .NET 
\fs24 string
\fs32 : 2 Bytes per Character and the "UTF-16" Reality\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 In .NET, a 
\fs24 string
\fs26  is an immutable sequence of 16-bit units (
\fs24 System.Char
\fs26 ).\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 The Common Myth:
\f1\b0  
\f3\i "A character in C# is always 2 bytes."
\f1\i0 \
\ls58\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 The Reality:
\f1\b0  While every char is indeed 2 bytes (16 bits), 
\f0\b one char does not always equal one Unicode character (Code Point)
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 For standard characters (like Latin alphabet, basic symbols), 1 code point = 1 char (2 bytes). This is the 
\f0\b BMP (Basic Multilingual Plane)
\f1\b0 .\
\ls58\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 For complex characters like emojis (e.g., \uc0\u55357 \u56960 ) or rare historical scripts, a single Unicode code point requires 
\f0\b two
\f1\b0  char values (4 bytes total). This is called a 
\f0\b Surrogate Pair
\f1\b0  (a High Surrogate followed by a Low Surrogate).\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 2. String Encodings: UTF-16 vs. UTF-8\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl0
\f2\b0\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 UTF-16 (Native to .NET):
\f1\b0  Uses 2 or 4 bytes per code point. Great for random access by index (though broken if surrogate pairs aren't handled), but wastes space for ASCII-heavy text.\
\ls59\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 UTF-8:
\f1\b0  Uses 1 to 4 bytes per code point. ASCII characters take only 1 byte. This is the universal standard for web APIs, JSON, and file storage.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f3\i\fs26 Conversion cost:
\f1\i0  Every time your .NET app reads a UTF-8 JSON payload from the web, .NET must decode those bytes into UTF-16 strings in memory, allocating fresh memory on the heap.\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 3. Unicode Problems: Orphaned Surrogates and How to Fix Them\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 Because UTF-16 splits characters across two 
\fs24 char
\fs26  units (High Surrogate and Low Surrogate), things can go wrong if a string is manipulated incorrectly.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 What is an Orphaned Surrogate?
\f1\b0  It occurs when a string gets cut in half right in the middle of a surrogate pair (e.g., via string.Substring(0, 5)), leaving a High Surrogate without its matching Low Surrogate, or vice versa. This creates invalid Unicode text that can crash serializers, databases, or UI renderers.\
\ls60\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 How to Fix It:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Validation:
\f1\b0  Use char.IsHighSurrogate() and char.IsLowSurrogate() checks before slicing strings.\
\ls60\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Safe Slicing:
\f1\b0  Avoid arbitrary character indexing on text containing emojis. Use StringInfo or ReadOnlySpan<char>with proper Rune iteration (System.Text.Rune).\
\ls60\ilvl1
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b\fs26 Replacement:
\f1\b0  When decoding malformed byte streams, replace invalid sequences with the Unicode Replacement Character (\\uFFFD / ) instead of throwing an exception.\
\pard\pardeftab560\sa40\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 4. Memory Impact and the Garbage Collector (GC)\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs26 \cf0 Strings are one of the leading causes of high GC pressure in .NET applications due to two main reasons:\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 A. Immutability\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 Once a string is created, its contents can 
\f0\b never
\f1\b0  change.\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 \cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 If you do string s = "Hello"; s += " World";, .NET does not modify "Hello". It allocates a 
\f0\b brand new string object
\f1\b0  on the Heap containing "Hello World", leaving the old "Hello" to be cleaned up by the GC.\
\ls61\ilvl0
\f2\fs18 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f1\fs26 Doing this in a loop creates massive amounts of garbage, triggering frequent Gen 0 and Gen 1 GC pauses.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 B. Solutions to Protect the GC\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls62\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf0 {\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b StringBuilder :
\f1\b0  For heavy string concatenation, StringBuilder uses an internal mutable buffer, avoiding heap allocations for every single concatenation step.\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Span<char> and ReadOnlySpan<char> :
\f1\b0  Allows you to slice, parse, and manipulate portions of strings (like parsing a large log file or JSON payload) 
\f0\b with zero heap allocations
\f1\b0 .\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b String Pooling / Interning (string.IsInterned) :
\f1\b0  If you have millions of identical short strings (like XML tags or status codes), string interning shares a single instance in memory across the entire app.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 XLSX topics : Restants regards\u8232 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://ADO.NET"}}{\fldrslt ADO.NET}}\uc0\u8232 Sous le capot, Entity Framework Core (EF Core) n'est qu'une 
\f0\b surcouche intelligente
\f1\b0  qui s'appuie directement sur ADO.NET.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Voici comment cela fonctionne en 3 \'e9tapes :\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls63\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Traduction :
\f1\b0  Vous \'e9crivez une requ\'eate en C# (LINQ). EF Core la traduit en code SQL.\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Ex\'e9cution via ADO.NET :
\f1\b0  EF Core utilise les classes bas niveau d'ADO.NET (DbConnection, DbCommand) pour ouvrir la connexion physique \'e0 la base de donn\'e9es et ex\'e9cuter le SQL.\
{\listtext	0.	}
\f0\b Mapping :
\f1\b0  ADO.NET r\'e9cup\'e8re les lignes de donn\'e9es brutes, et EF Core les transforme automatiquement en objets C# (vos entit\'e9s).\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 En clair : 
\f0\b EF Core vous \'e9vite d'\'e9crire la plomberie ennuyeuse, mais c'est bien ADO.NET qui fait le travail de communication avec la base de donn\'e9es en arri\'e8re-plan.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \u8232 - Typically used in conjunction with the IEnumerable interface, which represents a collection of objects that can be enumerated\
- The IEnumerator interface defines a single method called MoveNext, which is used to move to the next element in the collection. Also defines two properties, Current and Reset, which are used to access the current element in the collection and reset the enumerator to the beginning of the collection, respectively.\
- Example of using the IEnumerator interface to iterate over a collection fo strings:\
\
List<string> names = new List<string>() \{ "John", "Jane", "Bob" \};\
IEnumerator<string> enumerator = names.GetEnumerator();\
\
while (enumerator.MoveNext())\
\{\
    Console.WriteLine(enumerator.Current);\
\}\
\
- Above, the names list implements the IEnumerable interface, so it can be used with the GetEnumerator method to obtain a IEnumerator object\
- The IEnumerator object is then used in a while loop to iterate over the elements in the names list. The MoveNext() method is used to move to the next element in the collection, and the Current property is used to access the current element\
- IEnumerator is an important concept in .NET programming because it allows you to write code that can iterate over a collection of objects in a generic and reusable way\
\uc0\u8232 Ton intuition est bonne : avec une classe classique (non immutable), si 
\fs24 str2 = str1
\fs26 , modifier l'objet modifierait les deux.Mais avec les 
\fs24 string
\fs26 , c'est diff\'e9rent.\
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Regardons ce qui se passe pas \'e0 pas en m\'e9moire :\
\pard\pardeftab560\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
